# Onboarding — getting this repo deploying to the cluster

Two roles are involved: the **tenant** (you, building the app) and the
**operator** (whoever runs the cluster). The tenant fills in workloads; the
operator adds one wiring file to the cluster repo. The cluster repo's own
multi-repo onboarding page is authoritative for anything below; this page is the
tenant-shaped copy, so that what the operator is handed already names the right
objects.

Everything below is already rendered with this repo's answers: the slug is
`{{ app_slug }}`, the namespace is `{{ app_namespace }}` and the secret backend
is `{{ secrets_backend }}`.

---

## Tenant checklist

1. **Set the image** in `kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml`. The shipped value is a
   **placeholder no registry has**
   (`{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace | lower }}/{{ app_slug }}:REPLACE-ME`) — leave
   it and the first reconcile `ImagePullBackOff`s.
{%- if enable_image_build and ci_shape != 'none' %}
   CI builds the repo-root `Dockerfile` and pushes `:<short-sha>`, so that tag
   is the usual first edit.
{%- if ci_shape == 'github' and registry_host != 'ghcr.io' %}
   The vendored build workflow publishes to `ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>` from its
   own literals, so it does NOT push to `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:`
   at the GHCR path, or change the workflow's login step and its
   `REGISTRY`/`IMAGE_NAME`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' and not (registry_host.split(':')[0] == git_host or registry_host.split(':')[0].endswith('.' ~ git_host)) %}
   The build job pushes `$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:<short-sha>` — this project's GitLab
   registry, not `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:` at the project registry,
   or pass `registry:`, `login_registry:`, `login_user:` and `login_password:`
   to the `docker-build` include.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif enable_image_build %}
   This repo has no pipeline: `task build` builds the repo-root `Dockerfile`
   locally and you push the tag yourself, then point `image:` at it.
{%- else %}
   This repo builds nothing, so point it at the upstream image you run.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_image_build and secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}
   The registry is private, so the namespace also needs a pull credential —
   step O2b below.
{%- elif enable_image_build and secrets_backend != 'none' %}
   There is **no** O2b step below: `enable_registry_pull_secret` is off, so
   nothing gives the kubelet a credential. If the registry turns out to be
   private — both a GitLab project registry and a GHCR package are, by default,
   and a GHCR package's visibility does not follow its source repository's —
   the fix is `copier update --data enable_registry_pull_secret=true`, or make
   the package public.
{%- elif enable_image_build %}
   There is **no** O2b step below: the pull credential is itself an
   ExternalSecret, so a private registry needs a `secrets_backend` first. Make
   the package public, or add a backend and the credential in one
   `copier update`.
{%- elif secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}
   The upstream registry is private, so the namespace still needs a pull
   credential — step O2b below.
{%- endif %}
2. **Check the hostnames.** Public `{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` is
   ready in `ingressroute.yaml` and needs no operator action.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
   The internal `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` route ships too, and it
   DOES need one: step O5.
{%- else %}
   For a LAN/tailnet hostname as well, re-run `copier update` with
   `enable_internal_ingress: true` (it renders the route and its certificate
   together) and ask the operator for step O5.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend == 'none' %}
3. **Secrets.** This repo has none. If the app grows one, add an
   `ExternalSecret` referencing the store the operator provisions in step O3 —
   never a value in git.
{%- else %}
3. **Wire the secrets** in `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret.yaml`. It references
   the `ClusterSecretStore` `{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}`, which is what
   the operator creates in step O3 — the two names must match.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
   The item must carry a field named exactly as `property` spells it (`api-key`
   as shipped).
{%- else %}
   The project must carry a masked CI/CD variable named exactly as
   `remoteRef.key` spells it (`APP_API_KEY` as shipped); GitLab variable names
   allow only letters, digits and `_`.
{%- endif %}
   The Deployment's env binding is not `optional:`, so until the ExternalSecret
   syncs, the pod stays in `CreateContainerConfigError`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_servicemonitor %}
4. **Point observability** at the app's real metrics endpoint in
   `servicemonitor.yaml`, and keep `networkpolicy.yaml`'s scrape allow on the
   same port. A ServiceMonitor pointed at an endpoint that 404s scrapes
   `up == 0` and raises a TargetDown alert in the OPERATOR's Alertmanager.
{%- else %}
4. **Metrics are off.** When the app exposes `/metrics`, re-run `copier update`
   with `enable_servicemonitor: true` — it renders the ServiceMonitor and the
   matching scrape NetworkPolicy together.
{%- endif %}
5. `task lint`, commit on a branch, open a {{ change_request }}.
{%- if ci_shape == 'none' %}
   There is no pipeline here, so `task lint` and the pre-commit hooks are the
   whole gate.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
   CI must be green. The workflows are byte-identical library copies, so they
   cannot take an answer: set repository variable `K8S_VERSION` to
   `{{ k8s_version }}` under Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions. Until
   it is set, `flux-lint` warns and validates the manifests against the
   library's own fallback minor.
{%- else %}
   CI must be green.
{%- endif %}
{%- if forge == 'gitlab' %}
   Optional: create the project labels you want (Settings > Labels), then add a
   `/label ~<name>` line to the templates under `.gitlab/` to tag new issues and
   {{ change_request }}s automatically.
{%- endif %}
6. **Request wiring** from the operator: hand them this page. They need the slug
   (`{{ app_slug }}`), the namespace (`{{ app_namespace }}`), the secret backend
   (`{{ secrets_backend }}`), this repo's URL, whether an internal hostname is
   needed{% if secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}, the registry pull credential (O2b){% endif %}{% if enable_sso %} and the SSO objects (O8){% endif %}.
   Replace the `:REPLACE-ME` image tag first: the placeholder leaves the
   Deployment at zero available replicas and pages the operator.
7. After the operator merges the wiring, merge your `main`. Verify with
   `task flux:status` and `kubectl get pods -n {{ app_namespace }}` (kubeconfig
   from the operator).

---

## Operator checklist (performed in the cluster repo)

**Before the first tenant.** A cluster that has never onboarded a tenant may
still carry platform settings that were safe only while every route and every
ExternalSecret was operator-authored. Cross-namespace Traefik routing and the
Flux agent's RBAC are the usual two. Work the cluster repo's pre-onboarding
checklist first; its lint can fail on the first tenant entry until that is done.
The cluster-side objects this repo binds to are listed in `ARCHITECTURE.md`
under Platform contract. Merge the wiring only once the tenant repo carries a
real image tag: the shipped `:REPLACE-ME` placeholder holds the Deployment at
zero available replicas and `AppDown` (critical) fires in your Alertmanager ten
minutes later.
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}

A backend item missing the field the `ExternalSecret` names pages you the same
way: the pod's env binding is not `optional:`, so it never starts. Merge the
wiring once the item carries that field.
{%- endif %}

### O1 — Prepare the secret backend
{% if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
Create the items this tenant reads in the `{{ onepassword_vault }}` vault,
titled `"{{ app_slug }}: <Item>"`, and a scoped Connect token for them (find the
server ID with `op connect server list`, then `op connect token create
{{ app_slug }}-eso --server <ID> --vaults "{{ onepassword_vault }}"`).
{%- elif secrets_backend == 'gitlab' %}
Have the tenant create a project access token with the **Maintainer** role and
the `api` scope, on the GitLab project that holds these variables:
`{{ gitlab_api_url }}`. That project's numeric ID is the `projectID` in the store
below. Reading project CI/CD variables is a Maintainer-level operation, and the
ESO GitLab provider requires `api`, not `read_api`. Then set the masked CI/CD
variables this repo's ExternalSecret reads on that same project.
{%- else %}
Nothing to prepare — this tenant declares no secret backend. Skip O1, O2 and the
`ClusterSecretStore` in O3.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}

### O2 — Bootstrap the namespace secret

One-time, not Flux-managed, and re-runnable for a rotated token:

```bash
kubectl create namespace {{ app_namespace }} --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
kubectl -n {{ app_namespace }} create secret generic onepassword-connect-token \
  --from-literal=token=<CONNECT_TOKEN> --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- else %}
kubectl -n {{ app_namespace }} create secret generic gitlab-api-token \
  --from-literal=token=<PROJECT_ACCESS_TOKEN> --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
{%- endif %}
```

The pod reads the synced value into its environment at container start, so a
rotation also needs
`kubectl delete pod -n {{ app_namespace }} -l app.kubernetes.io/name={{ app_slug }}`.
A `rollout restart` is not a substitute: it writes a pod-template annotation the
cluster's kustomize-controller reverts on its next reconcile. The
`ExternalSecret` reports Ready either way.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' and enable_registry_pull_secret %}

### O2b — Registry pull credential

{% if registry_pull_host == 'ghcr.io' or registry_host == 'ghcr.io' -%}
A GHCR package is private by default and its visibility does **not** follow its
source repository's, so the kubelet cannot pull this tenant's image without a
credential.

1. Create a personal access token carrying the `read:packages` scope on an
   account that can read the package — GitHub's documented credential for
   `ghcr.io` is a **classic** token — and confirm that account (or the tenant
   repo) is granted access to the package itself, which is a separate grant
   from the source repository's.
2. Store the token and the **username it belongs to** (the GitHub account name)
   in the tenant's secret backend under the fields
   `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads. The username is half
   of what `docker login ghcr.io` takes, so it must be stored, not assumed.
{%- elif forge == 'gitlab' and (registry_host.split(':')[0] == git_host or registry_host.split(':')[0].endswith('.' ~ git_host)) -%}
A project registry is private by default, so the kubelet cannot pull this
tenant's image without a credential.

1. In the **tenant** project, create a deploy token scoped `read_registry`. The
   token *name* is not a secret; the value is.
2. Store the token and its **username** in the tenant's secret backend under the
   fields `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads — the forge
   generates the username (e.g. `gitlab+deploy-token-N`), so it must be stored,
   not assumed.
{%- else -%}
A private registry means the kubelet cannot pull this tenant's image without a
credential.

1. On `{{ registry_host }}`, create a read-only credential for this image — a
   robot account, deploy token or pull-scoped token, whatever the registry
   calls it.
2. Store the token and the **username it belongs to** in the tenant's secret
   backend under the fields
   `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret-registry.yaml` reads. The username is half of
   what `docker login` takes, so it must be stored, not assumed.
{%- endif %}

The image reference names `{{ registry_host }}`, and `{{ registry_pull_host }}`
is keyed into the same credential so the pull still authenticates on a cluster
whose nodes resolve that name to an internal registry. The kubelet matches a
pull secret against the literal host in the image reference, so if your nodes
must pull a different hostname, edit `image:` in
`kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml` — nothing rewrites it. Making the {% if registry_pull_host == 'ghcr.io' or registry_host == 'ghcr.io' %}package{% else %}registry{% endif %}
repository public avoids this step entirely.
{%- endif %}

### O3 — Add the wiring file

The file binds two roles to the tenant's ServiceAccount. `admin` normally covers
external-secrets.io and cert-manager.io through their aggregated `*-edit` roles;
`tenant-crd-editor` covers those two as well, so a cluster missing an aggregated
role still applies them. A RoleBinding naming a ClusterRole the cluster does not
have applies without error and grants nothing, so create that role first on a
cluster with no tenants yet. A cluster repo generated from
weisssrv-cluster-template ships it at
`kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/tenant-crd-editor.yaml`. On any other
cluster, create a ClusterRole granting edit on the API groups listed in
`ARCHITECTURE.md` § Platform contract.

`kubernetes/clusters/<cluster>/tenants/{{ app_slug }}.yaml`:

```yaml
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: {{ app_namespace }}
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: flux
    fluxcd.io/tenant: {{ app_slug }}
    # Pod Security Admission — baseline enforced, restricted advised.
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: baseline
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    # CRITICAL: no platform-store membership label here. It is what the
    # platform ClusterSecretStore admits, and it would hand this tenant every
    # item in the platform vault.{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %} This tenant gets its own store below.{% endif %}
---
# Cap the namespace: `admin` places no ceiling on what a tenant may request.
# `pods:` is the replica count plus the rollout surge, floored at 10;
# `limits.memory` must cover that many per-pod memory limits{% if enable_vpa %} at the VPA's maxAllowed{% endif %}.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-quota
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "{{ [replica_count | int + 2, 10] | max }}"
    requests.cpu: "5"
    requests.memory: 4Gi
    limits.memory: 12Gi
---
# Defaults and per-container ceilings, so a pod with no resources block cannot
# land unbounded inside the quota. A `max:` key with no matching `default:` key
# becomes the default limit, so CPU is left to the quota's requests.cpu.
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-limits
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:
        memory: 512Mi
      defaultRequest:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 128Mi
      max:
        memory: 2Gi
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
---
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: onepassword-{{ app_slug }}
spec:
  # A ClusterSecretStore is cluster-scoped: without `conditions`, ANY namespace
  # can reference it by name and read this store's vault. Scope every tenant
  # store to its own namespace.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - {{ app_namespace }}
  provider:
    onepassword:
      # The cluster's 1Password Connect Service; change it if yours differs.
      connectHost: http://onepassword-connect.external-secrets.svc.cluster.local:8080
      vaults:
        "{{ onepassword_vault }}": 1
      auth:
        secretRef:
          connectTokenSecretRef:
            name: onepassword-connect-token
            namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
            key: token
{%- elif secrets_backend == 'gitlab' %}
---
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: gitlab-{{ app_slug }}
spec:
  # A ClusterSecretStore is cluster-scoped: without `conditions`, ANY namespace
  # can reference it by name and read this store's variables. Scope every tenant
  # store to its own namespace, for every provider.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - {{ app_namespace }}
  provider:
    gitlab:
      # `url` is optional and DEFAULTS TO https://gitlab.com — omitting it sends
      # a self-hosted project token to the wrong instance, and the 401/404 that
      # follows names nothing in this file. It is the `gitlab_api_url` answer.
      url: {{ gitlab_api_url }}
      projectID: "<tenant project id>"
      auth:
        # Capital S: the GitLab provider is the one whose auth field is
        # `SecretRef` in the CRD. A lower-cased key is pruned as unknown,
        # leaving `auth` with no credential.
        SecretRef:
          accessToken:
            name: gitlab-api-token
            namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
            key: token
      # Variables scoped to a specific environment are invisible without this.
      environment: "*"
{%- endif %}
---
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: GitRepository
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 1m
  # A tenant repo may live anywhere Flux can clone from. For a private
  # repository add a `secretRef:` naming a deploy key or token secret.
  url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/{{ app_slug }}
  ref:
    branch: main
---
# A namespace-scoped ServiceAccount so kustomize-controller does NOT apply
# tenant manifests with its own cluster-admin. The SA lives in flux-system; the
# RoleBindings grant admin only in the tenant namespace.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
  namespace: flux-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux-admin
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# CRITICAL: `admin` does not aggregate traefik.io (IngressRoute) or
# monitoring.coreos.com (ServiceMonitor, PrometheusRule){% if enable_vpa %},
# nor autoscaling.k8s.io (VPA){% endif %}.
# Bind `tenant-crd-editor` as well, or the tenant Kustomization goes NotReady
# on the first IngressRoute.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}-flux-crd-editor
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_slug }}-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: tenant-crd-editor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: {{ app_slug }}
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  # Wait for the platform CRD chain so a fresh bootstrap does not apply this
  # tenant's CRs before the ESO, cert-manager and Traefik CRDs exist. Retarget
  # it at your own stage; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, Platform contract.
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
  serviceAccountName: {{ app_slug }}-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: {{ app_slug }}
  path: ./kubernetes/flux
  prune: true
  targetNamespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  wait: true
```

No `postBuild` block: nothing under `kubernetes/flux/` spells `${var}`, so this
repo needs no substitution. Add `postBuild.substituteFrom` only alongside a
placeholder that needs it, and write a literal dollar-brace as `$${var}`.
kustomize-controller usually runs with strict substitution, so one unresolvable
`${var}` fails the whole tenant reconcile. See `ARCHITECTURE.md`
§ Platform contract.

### O4 — Register it

Kustomize does not auto-discover, so add the file to the tenants
`kustomization.yaml`:

```yaml
resources:
  - tenant-crd-editor.yaml   # shipped by the cluster repo (see O3)
  - {{ app_slug }}.yaml      # add this line
```
{% if enable_internal_ingress %}
### O5 — Internal DNS

`{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` is not auto-provisioned — external-dns
manages the external zone only. Add the internal rewrite
(`{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` -> `{{ internal_vip }}`) to the cluster's
DNS inventory and run the DNS role. Public
`{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` needs nothing here.
{%- else %}
### O5 — Internal DNS

Not needed: this tenant publishes only the public hostname, which external-dns
provisions on its own.
{%- endif %}

### O6 — Persistent storage (only if the tenant needs a volume)

This template ships no PVC by design. Supply the StorageClass the cluster
offers, or provision the volume and a matching PV yourself, and hand the tenant
the PVC name. The tenant then adds the volume and its mount to
`deployment.yaml`{% if enable_vpa %} and sets `vpa.yaml` `updateMode: "Off"`{% endif %}.

On a Proxmox/ZFS cluster: add the zvol to the storage node's
`vm_additional_disks` in the cluster inventory, re-run the k3s play for that
host, and hand the tenant the PV/PVC plus the node-pin and NAS-toleration
pattern. NFS-backed volumes mount by hostname there, because the wildcard
certificate carries no IP SAN.

This app is stateless as generated. A volume provisioned here sits outside
every backup set until the operator adds it, and the operator owns its snapshot
and backup schedule.

### O7 — Reachability probe (recommended for user-facing routes)

This repo's `PrometheusRule` covers replica availability only. The platform
alerts cluster-wide on certificate expiry and on a `Certificate` that never
goes Ready. To also alert on end-to-end HTTP/TLS reachability, such as a broken
IngressRoute, certificate or DNS record while replicas stay ready, add
`{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` to the cluster's static blackbox target
list ({% if enable_sso %}`module: http_sso` for this SSO-gated route{% else %}`module: http_2xx`{% endif %}).
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
Add `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}` with `module: http_2xx` too. It
carries its own IngressRoute, its own certificate and the DNS record from O5,
and the external probe covers none of them.
{%- endif %}

### O8 — SSO objects

{% if enable_sso -%}
This repo's route references the forward-auth middleware, which does nothing on
its own. In the cluster's Authentik Terraform, add: a **proxy** provider whose
`external_host` is EXACTLY `https://{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}`, the
application bound to it, the access group, the policy binding, and the provider
on the embedded outpost's list. Then plan, review and apply supervised.

Two failure modes worth knowing first: a provider missing from the outpost's
list answers every request with a 404 from the outpost, and an `external_host`
set to the other split-horizon hostname breaks the redirect loop rather than
erroring.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}

The middleware is on the public route only. `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}`
stays source-filtered to the LAN and tailnet rather than authenticated, because
a proxy provider pins one `external_host`. Gating it too means a second provider
and a second outpost entry.
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
Not needed: SSO is off for this tenant and its route carries no forward-auth
middleware. `copier update --data enable_sso=true` renders the middleware
reference and brings this step back.
{%- endif %}

### O9 — Branch → {{ change_request }} → merge

Flux reconciles the new tenant on the next cycle.

### Removal

`git rm` the wiring file and its `kustomization.yaml` line → Flux prunes the
Kustomization, GitRepository{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %}, ClusterSecretStore{% endif %} and namespace. Then revoke the
tenant's token{% if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %} and delete the prefixed vault items{% endif %}. PVCs survive
(finalizers) and are cleaned up by hand.
